WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.938
Text input becomes safer when reading and parsing are separate, explicit steps.

2
00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:12.756
A bounded read, such as reading a line with std::getline into a std::string, avoids writing past a fixed buffer.

3
00:00:12.756 --> 00:00:17.282
But reading a line is not the same as validating it.

4
00:00:17.282 --> 00:00:32.096
The program still needs to check stream state, handle end of file, reject unexpected empty input when a value is required, and decide what to do with lines that are too large for the application policy.

5
00:00:32.096 --> 00:00:36.622
Stream extraction can be convenient, but it can also be ambiguous.

6
00:00:36.622 --> 00:00:44.029
A formatted extraction may stop early, leave trailing characters unread, or put the stream into a failed state.

7
00:00:44.029 --> 00:00:54.728
If code reads an integer and ignores the rest of the line, input such as a valid prefix followed by unexpected data may be accepted accidentally.

8
00:00:54.728 --> 00:01:04.603
Secure parsing uses explicit expectations: how many fields are allowed, which separators are valid, whether whitespace matters, and whether extra input should cause rejection.

9
00:01:04.603 --> 00:01:07.895
Trimming, normalization, and case handling should be intentional.

10
00:01:07.895 --> 00:01:14.890
Normalizing a username, path, command option, or identifier can change its meaning if the rules are vague.

11
00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:21.886
Reject partial or malformed records rather than trying to guess what the user meant in security-relevant contexts.

12
00:01:21.886 --> 00:01:33.819
A strong pattern is to read bounded text, parse into typed values, verify that parsing consumed exactly what the format required, and keep raw input separate from validated values.

13
00:01:33.819 --> 00:01:37.110
This keeps convenience from becoming ambiguity.
